Blind SQL Injection with Conditional Responses

Vulnerability: Blind SQL Injection
 Difficulty: Practitioner
 Platform: PortSwigger Web Security Academy

Goal

Find the administrator password and log in.

Steps I Followed

I intercepted the request and tested the TrackingId cookie.

True condition:

SQL
TrackingId=xyz' AND '1'='1

The Welcome back message appeared.

False condition:

SQL
TrackingId=xyz' AND '1'='2
 

The Welcome back message disappeared.

This confirmed a blind SQL injection vulnerability.

Next, I found the password length using the LENGTH() function. The administrator password length was 20.

Then I used:

SQL
TrackingId=xyz' AND (SELECT SUBSTRING(password,1,1)
FROM users
WHERE username='administrator')='a'--

Using Burp Intruder, I tested characters for each position of the password.

Whenever the Welcome back message appeared, I knew the character was correct.

I repeated this process until I found all 20 characters of the password.

Then I logged in as administrator and solved the lab.

Impact

An attacker could extract sensitive data from the database even when error messages are hidden.

What I Learned
How to identify blind SQL injection.
How to use true/false responses to extract data.
How to find password length using LENGTH().
How to extract characters using SUBSTRING().
How to automate the process with Burp Suite Intruder.
